<body>
<h1>Unit 1: Physical Quantities</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Mechanics</p><p class="summary">Before any law can be applied, a measurement has to be given a number, a unit, a direction where it matters, and an uncertainty. This unit fixes that vocabulary, and the 2019 SI redefinition moved the definition of the kilogram from a physical artefact to a fixed number.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>A physical quantity is a number multiplied by a unit; the unit is part of the quantity, not a label attached to it.</li><li>The SI defines seven base units (m, kg, s, A, K, mol, cd); every other unit is derived from them by definition, so the unit system is a language in which equations must be well-formed.</li><li>Since the 2019 redefinition the metre is fixed by c = 299 792 458 m/s exactly, the kilogram by the Planck constant h = 6.62607015e-34 J s exactly, and the kelvin by k = 1.380649e-23 J/K exactly.</li><li>Addition and subtraction demand matching dimensions; multiplication and division create new dimensions, which is why dimensional analysis can reject an equation before you compute anything.</li><li>Every measurement carries uncertainty: the final reported digit is uncertain, and the honest form of a result is best estimate plus or minus a bound.</li><li>Order-of-magnitude estimation (10^n) is a cheap sanity check: if the answer of a units question is 10^40, something has already gone wrong.</li><li>Dimensional homogeneity is a consistency test rather than a proof: an equation can be dimensionally perfect and still be wrong by a factor of two, a wrong sign, or an unaccounted dimensionless constant.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>Dimensional analysis only proves that an equation is dimensionally consistent, never that it is right; dimensionless constants such as pi, G and friction coefficients are invisible to the check.</li><li>The 'linear law' shape of Hooke's law is dimensionally consistent but only valid inside a small-strain regime, which is exactly the regime students assume without checking.</li><li>The modern kilogram is realised with Kibble and watt balances, so old textbooks that describe a platinum-iridium cylinder under lock and key are describing a definition that no longer holds.</li><li>Percentage errors are meaningful only for quantities measured on a ratio scale; a percentage change in temperature measured in Celsius is not comparable to the same change in kelvin.</li><li>The seven SI base units are a convention, not a discovery - a different set of definitions would describe the same physics, which is exactly why the 2019 redefinition changed the artefacts without changing a single law.</li></ul><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Speed of light (exact)</td><td>299 792 458 m/s</td></tr><tr><td>Planck constant (exact)</td><td>6.62607015e-34 J s</td></tr><tr><td>Boltzmann constant (exact)</td><td>1.380649e-23 J/K</td></tr><tr><td>Standard gravity</td><td>9.80665 m/s^2 (by definition)</td></tr><tr><td>Number of SI base units</td><td>7 (m, kg, s, A, K, mol, cd)</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>How small can the uncertainty of a realised kilogram become before metrology, rather than physics, sets the limit?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>Quantum metrology keeps tightening traceability, but no fundamental floor on repeated mass comparison has been identified.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Should the SI keep seven base units, or fold constants such as the Boltzmann constant into a smaller set?</b></p><p class="status">Under active scrutiny</p><p>The BIPM keeps this under periodic review; the debate is about definitional economy, not about new physics.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Express the speed of light, the Planck constant and the Boltzmann constant in base SI units and check that each is dimensionally M^0 L^0 T^0 Theta^0.</li><li>Your calculator reports g = 9.8 m/s^2. How many digits of that answer are trustworthy, and how would you justify your choice from the measurement alone?</li><li>Design a dimensional check that catches the classic mistake of adding a mass to a force.</li></ol><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://www.nist.gov/pml/owm/metric-si/si-units">NIST: SI Units</a></li><li><a href="https://physics.nist.gov/cuu/Uncertainty/basic.html">NIST: Measurement uncertainty</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/preface">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 1</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/1-4-dimensional-analysis">OpenStax: Dimensional Analysis</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/1-6-significant-figures">OpenStax: Significant Figures</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
</body>
